cff-version: 1.2.0
title: "Exoplanet Palette"
message: >-
  If you use this software or the colours it produces, please cite it as below — and please
  also cite the upstream sources it is built on, which are listed in LICENSE-DATA. In
  particular, the NASA Exoplanet Archive asks for a specific acknowledgement, reproduced
  verbatim in that file.
type: software
# NOTE: this is the public git identity, not a full name. Replace `name` with your real
# name (or swap to given-names / family-names) when you are ready to be cited by it —
# a citation file is a poor place to be pseudonymous, but a worse place to be wrong.
authors:
  - name: "jocarino"
    alias: jocarino
repository-code: "https://github.com/jocarino/roman-telescope-data"
abstract: >-
  The modelled visible colour of every known exoplanet, derived from physics. For each planet
  a geometric albedo spectrum is multiplied by its host star's spectrum and convolved with the
  CIE 1931 2-degree colour-matching functions to give a colour a human eye would see. Every
  planet is also shown as the Nancy Grace Roman Space Telescope's coronagraph would record it,
  reconstructed from that instrument's three supported visible bandpasses, so the perceptual
  distance between the two states how much colour identity survives a real filter set. Five
  solar-system planets are computed from measured albedo spectra as a calibration check.
  All colours are modelled, never photographed.
keywords:
  - exoplanets
  - astronomy
  - colour science
  - CIE 1931
  - geometric albedo
  - reflected light
  - Nancy Grace Roman Space Telescope
  - coronagraph
license: MIT
license-url: "https://github.com/jocarino/roman-telescope-data/blob/main/LICENSE"
references:
  - type: article
    title: "The NASA Exoplanet Archive and Exoplanet Follow-up Observing Program: Data, Tools, and Usage"
    authors:
      - family-names: Christiansen
        given-names: Jessie L.
    year: 2025
    journal: "The Planetary Science Journal"
    doi: 10.3847/PSJ/ade3c2
    notes: "Source of all planet and host-star parameters."
  - type: article
    title: "Exoplanet Albedo Spectra and Colors as a Function of Planet Phase, Separation, and Metallicity"
    authors:
      - family-names: Cahoy
        given-names: Kerri M.
      - family-names: Marley
        given-names: Mark S.
      - family-names: Fortney
        given-names: Jonathan J.
    year: 2010
    journal: "The Astrophysical Journal"
    doi: 10.1088/0004-637X/724/1/189
    notes: "Albedo model grid used by one of the spectrum engines."
  - type: article
    title: "Exoplanet Reflected-light Spectroscopy with PICASO"
    authors:
      - family-names: Batalha
        given-names: Natasha E.
    year: 2019
    journal: "The Astrophysical Journal"
    doi: 10.3847/1538-4357/ab1b51
    notes: "Radiative-transfer engine used for selected targets."
  - type: article
    title: "Methane, Ammonia, and Their Isotopologues in the Atmospheres of Uranus and Neptune"
    authors:
      - family-names: Karkoschka
        given-names: Erich
    year: 1998
    journal: "Icarus"
    doi: 10.1006/icar.1998.5913
    notes: "Measured giant-planet albedo spectra, via the NASA PDS Atmospheres Node."
  - type: article
    title: "A Comprehensive Spectroscopic Reference of the Solar System and Its Application to Exoplanet Direct Imaging"
    authors:
      - family-names: Payne
        given-names: A.
      - family-names: Villanueva
        given-names: G. L.
      - family-names: Kofman
        given-names: V.
    year: 2026
    journal: "The Planetary Science Journal"
    doi: 10.3847/PSJ/ae2feb
    notes: "Measured Earth albedo spectrum. Redistributed here under CC BY 4.0."
